+# K11 — outside-family re-derivation of Proposition 2.11 and P11
+
+## Five-line summary
+
+- **PROVED:** Proposition 2.11 is sound in the frozen promotion model under (H1)–(H5); I re-derived all six steps.
+- **PROVED:** Corollary 2.11a has the right beneficiary and timing for single passers and count-rule races reached by a move.
+- **COMPUTED-1:** A private kpsolve build returned exact results for 11 targeted cylinder positions; the independent checker confirmed the selected certificates.
+- **GAP:** A root race has no `W(X)` value in the current whole-board API; `cylcgt.value` instead sums its separated components.
+- **Recommendation:** Keep Proposition 2.11 **PROVED**; label P11 **PROVED conditionally for defined, certified roots**, and make its root-race exclusion or extension explicit.
+
+## 1. Re-derivation of Proposition 2.11 — **PROVED**
+
+Take a legal pre-promotion position on the cylinder. Fix beneficiary `β`, the other side `ω`, and a passed β-pawn `p` on file `f`. Use ρ-coordinates measured forward from β. Let `r` be p's current ρ-rank and let the strip be files `f−1, f, f+1` modulo the width. β uses this strategy: move `p` forward on every β turn, taking the initial double step when `r=2`.
+
+Write
+
+\[
+d=H-r-[r=2], \qquad e=\begin{cases}d,&s=\omega,\\d-1,&s=\beta.\end{cases}
+\]
+
+Here `d` is the number of β turns until p promotes; `e` counts ω turns before that promotion. The opponent-only game used in (H5) deletes p, leaves β's other pawns and both kings fixed as blockers, and permits the initial en passant capture only when ω is already to move. Let `μ` be its shortest ω-pawn promotion time, including the promoting move. The certificate assumes: p's route is clear and p is e.p.-safe; β's king has no adjacent pawn-file; ω has the permanently safe king move from (H3); β's other pawns beside p are no more than one ρ-step ahead; and `μ ≥ e+1`.
+
+### 1.1 Why the play is safe
+
+Before any promotion, files cannot acquire pawns from nowhere. A pawn changes file only by capturing a pawn on the destination file; an e.p. capture also lands on the captured pawn's file. Consequently an initially pawn-free file remains empty. A king move ends the frozen game against its maker, and a promotion ends it for its maker. Along any line not already won by an ω king move, both kings therefore remain on their original squares.
+
+No pawn can ever enter a file beside β's king: those files are empty at the root by (H2), and the file fact just proved keeps them empty. Thus no pawn attacks β's king. The fixed kings are nonadjacent by legality of `y`, so β is not in check. In particular, p's clear push is never barred by king safety.
+
+For ω, take the square `t` supplied by (H3). It is empty because its file and the two neighboring files stay pawn-free; it is not beside β's fixed king; and no β-pawn attacks it. It is a legal neighboring king square even when ω's king is currently checked. Hence ω cannot be stalemated during the strategy. If ω chooses a king move, that move immediately loses in this model.
+
+### 1.2 The strip invariant
+
+At each β-to-move position, (Jβ) says every ω-pawn in the strip has ρ at most `r+1`. At each ω-to-move position, (Jω) says every such pawn has ρ at most `r`, and p has no available e.p. capture. At all positions, (I1) says every square farther along p's file is empty. At the root, the passed-pawn and clear-route clauses of (H1) put every ω-pawn in the strip at or behind p, rule out e.p. capture of p, and give (I1). This establishes whichever turn invariant applies initially.
+
+Suppose β pushes p from rank `r` to `r′`.
+
+- A single push has `r′=r+1`; (Jβ) leaves every ω-pawn at ρ≤`r′`. No e.p. right is created.
+- A double push has `r′=r+2`; every ω-pawn in the strip is still at most `r+1`, so it is strictly behind the new p. An e.p. capture of p would require an ω-pawn on p's new rank, `r+2`, which is absent. Thus the new p cannot be taken e.p. after its own double step. The new position also satisfies (Jω).
+
+In either case, the squares p crossed were empty by (I1), and (I1) remains true beyond p's new square.
+
+Now let ω make a pawn move. A push or double step reduces ρ, so it cannot run into p or occupy a square ahead of p on file `f`. A capture lands on the square of a β-pawn. If that destination is file `f`, (I1) says the victim is behind p. If the destination is `f−1` or `f+1`, the victim is one of β's stationary pawns covered by (H4); its rank is at most the original `r₀+1`, hence at most the current `r+1`. This covers captures into the strip from outside it, captures wholly inside it, and captures that leave the strip: a pawn leaving contributes no new strip occupant, while any pawn entering lands on one of the bounded β-pawn squares. So (Jβ) is restored and (I1) is preserved.
+
+The only e.p. right ω can use is the right present at the root when `s=ω`; it expires after this first ω move. Its victim cannot be p by (H1). If the victim is on file `f`, it is behind p; if on a neighboring file, (H4) bounds its rank by `r₀+1`. The e.p. landing square is one rank behind that victim in β's coordinates, so it also obeys the strip bound and cannot be ahead of p. If `s=β`, an initial right belongs to β and is discarded when β plays p; it is not an ω move in the opponent-only game.
+
+These cases establish (Jω), (Jβ), and (I1) after every possible pawn move. In particular, the invariant accounts for a capture that deposits an ω-pawn beside p; the pawn need not have advanced toward p to get there.
+
+### 1.3 Preservation, simulation, and timing
+
+At an ω turn, a normal capture of p would need an ω-pawn beside p at ρ=`r+1`; (Jω) excludes it. The initial e.p. capture is excluded by (H1), and a new e.p. capture after p's double step is excluded by the rank argument above. No pawn can block p because (I1) keeps its file ahead clear. A king capture, if available, is part of a king move and loses for ω immediately. Thus no non-losing ω play captures or blocks p, while β always has its prescribed legal push.
+
+Every ω pawn move made in the real play is available in the opponent-only game: deleting p can remove an obstruction but cannot invalidate a real move; all other β pawns and both kings remain fixed. The only possible initial e.p. capture is the same one in both plays, and later rights cannot create an additional ω capture of p. Hence the ω pawn sequence from the real play is a path in that game. If ω promotes in `k` real pawn moves, then `k≥μ`. A king move instead loses at once.
+
+If ω starts, before β's `d`-th push ω has moved exactly `d` times, so `e=d`. If β starts, there is one extra β push at the beginning; before the `d`-th push ω has moved `d−1` times, so `e=d−1`. In both orders, (H5) gives `μ≥e+1`; therefore ω cannot have promoted during those `e` turns. By (H3), no intervening ω turn can end in stalemate. β's `d`-th push is legal and promotes, which wins immediately. This proves the strategy in both move orders.
+
+### 1.4 Step-by-step verdicts
+
+| LOOPY step | Verdict | Re-derived point |
+|---|---|---|
+| 0 — rules of the frozen model | **SOUND** | Before promotion there are only pawns and fixed kings; king moves and promotions have the stated terminal results, and empty files persist. |
+| 1 — king safety | **SOUND** | (H2) and the empty-file fact prevent every pawn check on β; (H3) supplies a legal, unattacked ω-king destination. |
+| 2 — (Jβ), (Jω), (I1) | **SOUND** | The induction covers pushes, double steps, ordinary captures into/within/out of the strip, the root e.p. right, and the non-capturability of p after its own double step. |
+| 3 — p survives and can advance | **SOUND** | Jω bars an ordinary capture; H1 and the double-step case bar e.p.; I1 bars blockage. |
+| 4 — ω's play is in Gω | **SOUND** | Every real ω-pawn move remains legal with p removed; other blockers and the only usable initial e.p. right match. |
+| 5 — race to promotion | **SOUND** | The counts are `e=d` when ω starts and `e=d−1` when β starts; H5 leaves the promoting move to β. |
+
+**Legality of `y` — PROVED necessary for the statement as used.** `y` is a legal position before promotion: no overlaps, kings nonadjacent, and the side not to move is not in check. (H2) itself rules out check on β. If ω is to move while checked, (H3)'s square gives a legal evasion; if ω is not to move and is checked, `y` would be illegal. The en passant marker, when present, must be the valid right from the immediately preceding double step. These are the stored-position rules in `kpsolve` as well as the chess legality conditions.
+
+**Why H≥5 — PROVED as the uniform-proof boundary, not a necessary condition for the result.** At height four, a pawn on its start rank promotes by its first double step. The generic induction above treats a β push as leading to another ω-to-move position, so that terminal move must be split off. For H≥5 a start-rank double step stays on the board as a pawn and the invariant induction is uniform. At H=4, every legal β-pawn promotion distance is one: if β moves, p promotes immediately; if ω moves first, (H5) says ω needs at least two moves, after which β promotes on its first turn. Thus a separate one-move case extends the conclusion to H=4; H≥5 is the clean domain for the stated proof, not a sharp truth boundary.
+
+## 2. Corollaries — **PROVED**
+
+### 2.11a: beneficiary and move order
+
+Let M make the move that reaches the terminal and let N be next to move. `cylcgt.pawns._child` gives a one-sided passer to its owner. For a race under `RACE_RULE="count"`, it compares `promote_distance` for the two sides and gives the terminal to N on a tie: its code chooses N exactly when `dp(N) ≤ dp(M)`. With p a passer of the credited side β, the timing in Proposition 2.11 is:
+
+| Child terminal | Credited side β | Who moves first in the certificate? | `e` |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| Only M has a passer | M | N, the opponent | `d` |
+| Only N has a passer | N | β itself | `d−1` |
+| Race, `dp(N)≤dp(M)` | N | β itself | `d−1` |
+| Race, `dp(N)>dp(M)` | M | N, the opponent | `d` |
+
+This checks both move orders for each type of child terminal. The race rule is invoked only when a move creates the race; its tie rule is not a root-race convention. The certificate's `μ` ranges over all ω pawns, including non-passers, so (H5) can be stricter than the race rule's comparison of fastest passed pawns.
+
+The adopted passed-pawn test is important here: `PASSED_RESPECTS_EP=True` prevents an e.p.-capturable pawn from being used as p. The initial e.p. capture against a different β-pawn is allowed in Gω when ω moves first and is counted in μ.
+
+### 2.11b: parked kings on cyl:8×6
+
+With kings on a1/a6 and pawns confined to c–g, no pawn can enter a, b, or h. The squares adjacent to either king's file stay pawn-free, proving (H2). For an ω king on a6 choose a5; for ω on a1 choose a2. Each lies on a pawn-free three-file band and is four king-steps from the other king, proving (H3), including when ω is checked. The kings cannot lie on p's file, so H1's king-clearance clause is automatic. For each candidate beneficiary, the remaining checks are that its selected front pawn is genuinely passed and clear under the e.p.-aware test, (H4) for friendly pawns on neighboring files, and (H5) against all opponent pawns.
+
+## 3. P11 as amended — conditional proof plus a root-race gap
+
+**PROVED for the stated non-race roots, subject to all stated certificates and (E).** Theorem 1.14(i) supplies `P(X)=D(X)` once (V1)–(V3) hold. In this layout, pawn moves cannot cross the empty a/b/h files; pawns on neighboring occupied files make exactly the corresponding pawn-component options. The parked kings do not check any pawn because no pawn can reach b or h. For the component formulation used in Theorem 1.14, condition (E) must hold at every pre-event node: no reachable position with at least two components may have a usable e.p. right. The layout alone does not imply (E).
+
+There is no stalemate before a decisive event: the appropriate king always has a legal move to a2 or a5. In the frozen model that king move is an immediate loss for its maker; it is the matching king-group suicide in D, so no separate passer certificate is needed for it. Every passer or race terminal reached by a move is handled by Corollary 2.11a when its chosen β-pawn passes H1–H5. A non-race root with a single side's passer is handled by Proposition 2.11 itself, with β the side that W credits. These are precisely the V2 cases Theorem 1.14 needs. HSS is not needed for `o(W(X))`; it is the additional condition for the separate `o(S(X))` claim via Theorems 1.11(c) and 1.14(ii).
+
+**GAP if root races are included.** The current whole-board `W` implementation rejects a race already present at the root (`lemmas/common.py: W` raises `RaceError`). `cylcgt.pawns.value` does something different: for separated root passers it sums the components and returns outcome P in the examples below; `_child` applies the count rule only to a race created by a move. So `o(W(X))` is undefined for a root race in the current API. Proposition 2.11 can still certify the frozen result at such a root if β is chosen and H1–H5 hold, but it cannot establish equality to an undefined `o(W(X))`. Repair P11 by either excluding root races explicitly, or defining a root count convention (naturally, side to move is first, ties to it) and adding that convention to W. The phrase “if X already has a passer” should mean “if exactly one side has a passer” unless the latter extension is added.
+
+**Root check — no gap inside P11; it is excluded geometrically.** With kings on a1/a6 and pawns only on c–g, neither king can be checked. Outside that layout, the no-check part of (V1) matters. The checked position `Ke2 ka6 Pc4 Pf2 pf3 w -` has a White passer at c4 and no Black passer; W therefore credits White. White is to move in check from f3, and its f2 pawn is blocked by f3, so its only evasions are king moves, which lose in `--frozen`. kpsolve returns `L L` (Black wins) on it. This is a counterexample to dropping the no-check hypothesis, not to P11's cylinder layout.
+
+**Root e.p. rights and (E).** Proposition 2.11 handles a usable initial ω e.p. capture when `s=ω`, provided it does not capture p; that capture is the first edge of Gω and is included in μ. If β is to move, the prescribed p-push expires an e.p. right belonging to β. Separately, (E) protects the move correspondence when the board is represented as a sum of components: a usable right in one component expires globally if a different component is played, but would persist in the isolated component game. A root with such a right and multiple components fails (E), even if Proposition 2.11 certifies its passer. Thus (E) is a separate hypothesis of the Theorem 1.14 route, not something implied by H1–H5. A direct proof against whole-board W might weaken it, but that would be a different proof.
+
+**Root with no terminal certificate.** The in-layout root `Ka1 ka6 Pc4 Pe2 pf3 b -` is a useful control. W sees White's c4 passer and awards White. For p=c4, the independent checker gets `e=2`, `μ=2`, so H5 fails (`μ` would have to be at least 3); kpsolve returns `L L`, Black wins by creating a faster race. This confirms that the root certificate cannot be dropped.
+
+## 4. Computations — **COMPUTED-1**
+
+The checker `k11_check.py` is 187 lines, imports no K10 material, and computes μ by its own breadth-first search over ω-pawn moves with fixed kings and β-pawns. It checks the initial e.p. case, the two terminal-race move orders, root-race API behavior, and the H5-failing in-layout root. Its exact command is:
+
+```sh
+PYTHONPATH=. .venv/bin/python kings/delegate/out/K11/k11_check.py
+```
+
+For the root e.p. example `Ka1 ka6 Pc4 Pe3 Pg4 pd4 b c3`, the script found H1–H5 all true, `d=2`, `e=2`, `μ=3`, and two pawn components with a usable e.p. right. This is an example where (E) fails at the root even though Proposition 2.11's local certificate holds. For a race made by White, it found `dp(W/B)=(2,3)`, β=White=M, and `(d,e,μ)=(2,2,3)`; for the race made by Black it found the same distances, β=White=N, and `(d,e,μ)=(2,1,3)`. For the uncertified P11 root, it found H1–H4 true but H5 false with `μ=e=2`.
+
+The solver was built privately and the 11-position input was run once through the shared lock. Reproduce with:
+
+```sh
+cc -O2 -o kings/delegate/out/K11/kp kings/kpsolve.c -lpthread
+zsh kings/delegate/out/K11/run_examples.sh
+```
+
+`run_examples.sh` writes its input and output under this directory, calls `kings/delegate/heavy.sh kings/delegate/out/K11/kp --board cyl:8x6 --frozen --cap 1 --threads 2 < in.txt > out.txt`, prints the output, then removes both batch files. kpsolve reported 126 layers, 580,608 states, depth 6, 0.1 seconds; all 11 A/B bounds agreed, so all 11 lines below are exact. Its results are from White's point of view (`L L` means Black wins).
+
+| Input | kpsolve | Reading |
+|---|---|---|
+| `Ka1 ka6 Pc4 pf4 w -` | `W W` | Root race; White is faster (2 vs 3). |
+| `Ka1 ka6 Pc4 pf4 b -` | `W W` | Same root race, Black to move. |
+| `Ka1 ka6 Pc4 pf2 w -` | `L L` | Root race; Black is faster (1 vs 2). |
+| `Ka1 ka6 Pc4 pf2 b -` | `L L` | Same root race, Black to move. |
+| `Ka1 ka6 Pc3 Pd4 pf4 b -` | `W W` | Count-rule race after White's move; White wins. |
+| `Ka1 ka6 Pc4 pd4 w -` | `W W` | Count-rule race after Black's move; White wins. |
+| `Ka1 ka6 Pd4 w -` | `W W` | Single White passer created by White. |
+| `Ka1 ka6 Pc4 Pe3 pd4 w -` | `W W` | Single White passer credited to White after Black's move. |
+| `Ka1 ka6 Pc4 Pe3 Pg4 pd4 b c3` | `W W` | Certified root passer with usable initial e.p. capture against another pawn. |
+| `Ka1 ka6 Pc4 Pe2 pf3 b -` | `L L` | Root certificate fails H5; W still gives White the root passer. |
+| `Ke2 ka6 Pc4 Pf2 pf3 w -` | `L L` | Side to move is checked outside P11's layout. |
+
+The wrapper emitted `nice: setpriority: Operation not permitted` on this machine, then completed the small batch normally. The shared lock was used; the solver's reported run time was 0.1 s. Peak RSS was not measured. The input/output batches were removed; only this report and the two small scripts plus the private solver binary remain.
+
+## 5. Recommended labels
+
+- **Proposition 2.11: PROVED**, as a sufficient certificate in the frozen promotion model, conditional on H1–H5 and legal `y`. All Steps 0–5 are sound. This says nothing about real, movable kings.
+- **Corollary 2.11a: PROVED**, for a passer or race terminal reached by a move and the count rule as implemented.
+- **Corollary 2.11b: PROVED**, for the geometric clauses in the stated 8×6 layout; each candidate still needs the stated passer, H4, and H5 checks.
+- **P11: PROVED conditionally for non-race roots** when every move-reached passer/race terminal and any single-passer root satisfy the certificate, and (E) holds. As worded if it includes a root race, mark **GAP** until the race is excluded or W's root-race rule is defined. The `o(S)` conclusion additionally needs HSS and the tempo bound.
